  "full_response": "TCP (Transmission Control Protocol) and UDP (User Datagram Protocol) are both fundamental protocols facilitate the transfer of data across networks, yet they have some key differences which make them suitable for different kinds of applications. Below are the primary differences between the two:\n\n1. Connection-oriented vs. Connectionless:\n   - TCP: A connection-oriented protocol, which means that a connection must be established between the source and destination before data can be sent. This connection remains active for the duration of the communication.\n   - UDP: A connectionless protocol; no connection is established between the source and destination before any data transfers are performed. Packets are sent independently of one another.\n\n2. Reliability:\n   - TCP: Ensures a high level of reliability, as it has built-in mechanisms for guaranteeing that the data reaches its destination. TCP retransmits lost packets and uses acknowledgments and retransmissions to ensure data accuracy.\n   - UDP: Offers a lower level of reliability, as it does not have any built-in mechanisms for packet retransmission, order guarantees, or data integrity. The onus of reliability depends on the application using it.\n\n3. Order:\n   - TCP: Order guarantees the packets are delivered to and from their respective application processes in the same order they were sent.\n   - UDP: Does not guarantee that packets will arrive in the ordered sequence. So if an application receives packets out of order, it's often up to the application to handle reordering if necessary.\n\n4. Flow Control:\n   - TCP: Implements flow control mechanisms to regulate the amount of data being sent and received, thus preventing congestion and overloading at the receiver's endpoint.\n   - UDP: Does not provide flow control, which means that it is up to the application to manage the sending and receiving of data.\n\n5. Overhead:\n - TCP: Due to its reliability and flow control mechanisms, TCP introduces more overhead in the form of acknowledgments, sequence numbers, and error-checking data.\n   UDP: Has less overhead than TCP, making it a more efficient protocol for applications that require certain performance characteristics. However, because it does not guarantee any negative service, many applications have to build their own error-handling and data integrity measures.\n\nDue to these differences, TCP and UDP are typically used in different networking scenarios. TCP's connection-oriented and reliable nature makes it a good fit for applications that require guaranteed data delivery, such as web browsing, email, and file transfers. UDP's connectionless and low-overhead characteristics make it ideal for real-time applications, such as online",
